{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792173487,"nonce":"pi0FT4Tw8BeQXanuXehl0wbZPFU=","title":"Local clip's antialiasing fringe may not match the screen clip's","message":"Flagged while answering a blinc_ts review question about a related\nfix there (blinc_abi git-bug 77fc7dc4, cut-rounded-clip).\n\nSdf.hx's clipCoverage (the merged screen clip, used by every box/text/\nimage/backdrop shader) hardcodes its antialiasing fringe:\nsmoothstep(-0.75, 0.75, ...), i.e. 0.75 layout units, matching what\nblinc_ts calls its own screen-clip convention. localClipCoverage (the\nper-record local clip, rows 6/7, gated only on `clips \u003e 1.5` with no\ntransform check) instead takes `aa` from the caller, and BoxShader.hx\npasses `halfPixel(local)` — a screen-space-derivative-based \"half a\npixel\", measured in the record's own coordinates.\n\nFor an identity or translation-only record those two fringes should\nbe comparable in principle, but derivative-based half-a-pixel is\nnarrower than 0.75 layout units whenever pixelRatio \u003e 1 (at 2x, half\na device pixel is about 0.25 layout px). blinc_ts hit exactly this:\na local-clipped edge sharpened as it crossed from a screen clip to a\nlocal clip (e.g. a rounded avatar cut by a scroll view's edge), and\nfixed it by making their local clip use the same 0.75-layout-px\nfringe their screen clip does.\n\nNot yet confirmed visually in ashui — this is a static-reading lead,\nnot a verified bug. Next step: a render pixel-diff scene with a\nrounded, overflow-hidden element partly clipped by an ancestor's\nrect, at 2x pixelRatio, compared against 1x, checking for a visible\nhardening of the cut edge.","files":null}]}